.TH ARMSTAT 8 "August 22, 2026" "armstat" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
armstat \- ARM server monitoring tool in the style of turbostat
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B armstat
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B armstat
collects interval-based CPU, idle, power, temperature, PMU, and topology data
on ARM64 servers.

It uses Linux interfaces such as
.BR /proc/stat ,
.BR sysfs ,
.BR hwmon ,
.BR thermal_zone ,
and
.BR perf_event_open (2).

The current output model is:
.IP \(bu 2
default mode: one \fBSUM\fR row plus per-package aggregation rows
.IP \(bu 2
\fB-a\fR mode: \fBSUM\fR row + per-package aggregation rows + per-CPU rows
.IP \(bu 2
summary mode (\fB-S\fR): one \fBSUM\fR row per interval
.IP \(bu 2
explicit \fB-s\fR selection emits only scopes requested by the selected groups
or exact fields; use \fBcpu\fR to request CPU rows and \fBpkg\fR to request
package rows

.PP
When \fB--cpu\fR is active, the default aggregate rows are based on the filtered
tracked CPU set, while platform/global fields keep their natural summary scope.
If CPU rows are explicitly expanded, implicit aggregate rows are suppressed to
avoid surprising mixed-scope output. Use \fB-S --cpu\fR for an explicitly
filtered summary.
.PP
The first visible line always represents one complete sampling interval, not an
instantaneous snapshot at process start.
.PP
Later samples use monotonic deadlines anchored to the baseline. Normal
collection and formatting overhead is deducted from the following wait instead
of accumulating as cadence drift. If work overruns a full interval, missed
deadlines are skipped rather than followed by burst catch-up samples; metric
calculations continue to use the measured interval delta.
.PP
If armstat rebuilds runtime state after CPU topology change, that sample
becomes a new baseline and is not printed as a normal interval row.

.SH SUPPORTED PLATFORM
The initial supported hardware family is Huawei Kunpeng running ARM64 Linux.
Other ARM64 systems may expose compatible Linux interfaces, but they are not
part of the initial support claim. Each Kunpeng model claimed for deployment
must pass the target acceptance test and a sustained soak run on that model.

.SH OPTIMIZATION STRATEGY
The implementation is optimized around interval sampling overhead:

.IP \(bu 2
static / rebuild work handles CPU inventory, topology, sensor discovery, and
cpuidle state metadata
.IP \(bu 2
the per-interval hotplug check compares the compact Linux online-CPU mask and
only enumerates all \fBcpuN\fR directories after a change or read failure
.IP \(bu 2
slow-changing CPU attributes such as min/max frequency, governor, boost, and
cpuidle disable flags are refreshed with a rolling budget instead of full
machine sweeps, and cpufreq data is skipped entirely when no frequency field
is visible
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/proc/stat\fR is parsed only when selected Busy/Idle/IOWait, LPI residency,
or system-counter fields require it, then reused by all such consumers in that
interval; usage-only LPI output does not require it
.IP \(bu 2
cpuidle reads are selected independently by output scope, visible state index,
and counter family: summary residency does not read \fBstateN/usage\fR, while
\fBLPI-N_usage\fR alone does not read \fBstateN/time\fR
.IP \(bu 2
power, energy, and temperature sensors are only sampled when a visible field
depends on them
.IP \(bu 2
PMU counters are opened as perf groups per tracked CPU and multiplexed
intervals are scaled using \fBtime_enabled\fR / \fBtime_running\fR
.IP \(bu 2
summary-only and package-only output do not materialize unused per-CPU rows
.IP \(bu 2
before normal interval sampling, armstat makes a best-effort attempt to lower
its own nice value, matching turbostat's intent to reduce monitor-induced
scheduling jitter; failure is non-fatal
.IP \(bu 2
the default Busy/Idle policy is \fBauto\fR: use \fB/proc/stat\fR on ordinary
CPUs and prefer \fB/proc/schedstat\fR runtime accounting on CPUs listed in
\fB/sys/devices/system/cpu/nohz_full\fR. The \fBtask-clock\fR mode is retained
as a compatibility alias and currently resolves to the same implementation as
\fBschedstat\fR

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-i ", " \-\-interval " seconds"
Sampling interval in finite positive seconds, with a minimum of 0.000001
seconds (one microsecond). Default: 1.0. The text startup banner preserves
subsecond precision
.TP
.BI \-n ", " \-\-num-iterations " n"
Number of completed samples to print. Zero means unlimited and is the default
.TP
.BI \-N ", " \-\-header-iterations " n"
Reprint the text header after every N data rows
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-cpu " cpu-list"
Monitor only the specified real CPU IDs. Accepts comma-separated values and
ranges such as \fB0,1,4-7\fR. This is a sampling filter: only online matching
CPUs become tracked CPUs, so per-CPU rows, summary averages, PMU groups, and
per-CPU Busy/Idle/sysfs readers are based on the filtered tracked set. Invalid
tokens, reversed ranges, empty tokens, and filters that match no online CPU are
startup errors. The current build represents CPU IDs 0 through 1023; higher
IDs cannot be selected
.TP
.BI \-B ", " \-\-busy-source " auto|procstat|schedstat|task-clock"
Select the authoritative Busy/Idle source. Default: \fBauto\fR
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output " file"
Write output to file
.TP
.BI \-O ", " \-\-export " file"
Export text, JSON, or CSV output to file. This is an explicit alias of
\fB-o\fR / \fB--output\fR and is especially convenient for machine-readable
formats. Current JSON/CSV exports carry \fBschema_version=8\fR. The detailed
export contract is documented in \fBREFERENCE.md\fR and
\fBREFERENCE.zh-CN.md\fR
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress the interval banner and text headers
.TP
.B \-D, \-\-dump
Wait for, collect, and print one complete interval, then exit. Text output
keeps its banner and column header unless \fB-q\fR is also specified
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-summary
Summary-only mode. Output one \fBSUM\fR row/object per interval
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-all
Enable all supported base column groups and expand the default aggregate view
with per-CPU rows. PMU and IPC remain explicit and are not auto-enabled by
\fB-a\fR
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-format " text|json|csv"
Select output format. Default: text
.TP
.BI \-s ", " \-\-show " items"
Show only the specified column groups or exact field names. Unknown items
are reported as errors. Repeated \fB-s\fR options form a union. Explicit
\fB-p\fR, \fB-I\fR, and \fB-J\fR requests remain in that union regardless of
their position relative to \fB-s\fR. After platform discovery, a selection
that cannot produce any row in the chosen mode is a startup error
.TP
.BI \-H ", " \-\-hide " items"
Hide the specified column groups or exact field names. Unknown items are
reported as errors, matching \fB\-\-show\fR behavior
.TP
.BI \-p ", " \-\-pmu-events " events"
Enable PMU events. Events are comma-separated. Unknown named events and lists
with duplicate names or more than \fBMAX_PMU_EVENTS\fR entries are startup
errors; known events that cannot be opened because of perf permissions remain
visible as unavailable values
.TP
.B \-I, \-\-ipc
Enable PMU \fBcycles,instructions\fR and show IPC columns
.TP
.B \-J, \-\-joules
Show interval energy in Joules
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-list
List built-in column groups, exact selectable fields with scope, data type,
unit, text label, and JSON key, and built-in PMU event names. Stable field IDs
are recommended for scripts
.TP
.B \-P, \-\-probe
Print a one-shot capability summary for the current platform, including CPU
topology, effective busy-source policy, cpuidle/LPI availability,
the temperature node mask, selected package-power and memory-bandwidth sysfs
paths, source candidate counts and ambiguity notes, and basic PMU availability.
When cpuidle states are present, \fBidle_state_N_name\fR maps each visible
\fBLPI-N\fR field to the corresponding Linux \fBstateN/name\fR value.
The PMU check opens \fBcycles\fR on the
first tracked CPU only and is not a full event-by-CPU validation. The
key-value contract reports \fBprobe_schema_version: 1\fR
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-debug
Enable debug output
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Show version information
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show help

.SH COLUMN GROUPS
The following aliases are understood by
.B --show
and
.BR --hide :

.IP \(bu 2
\fBcpu\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpkg\fR, \fBpackage\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcore\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnuma\fR, \fBnode\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfreq\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBidle\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpower\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtemp\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpmu\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsysstat\fR, \fBirq\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmembw\fR, \fBmem\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBipc\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBenergy\fR, \fBjoules\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBall\fR

.PP
\fB--show\fR and \fB--hide\fR also accept exact field names such as
\fBIdle%\fR, \fBBusy%\fR, \fBIOWait%\fR, \fBSoftIRQs\fR, \fBLPI-0\fR, or
\fBPower\fR.

.SH PMU EVENTS
Built-in PMU names include:

.IP \(bu 2
\fBcycles\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBinstructions\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcache-references\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcache-misses\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbranches\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbranch-misses\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmem-access\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmem-read\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmem-write\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBl1d-cache-refill\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBl1d-cache\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBl1i-cache-refill\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBl1i-cache\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBl2d-cache-refill\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBl2d-cache\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBl3d-cache-refill\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBl3d-cache\fR

The ARMv8 raw aliases use architectural PMUv3 event numbers. In particular,
\fBmem-read\fR counts retired loads and \fBmem-write\fR counts retired stores;
neither is a byte or memory-bandwidth counter. Optional cache events may be
unsupported by a particular CPU.

PMU counters are opened per tracked CPU. Events on the same CPU are grouped
with perf group semantics, and multiplexed intervals are scaled using
\fBtime_enabled\fR / \fBtime_running\fR when available. PMU file descriptor
use scales with both the event count and tracked CPU count; reduce either on
large systems if descriptor pressure is a concern. Before opening PMU groups,
armstat best-effort raises its soft \fBRLIMIT_NOFILE\fR within the existing
hard limit and warns if the resulting budget may still be insufficient.

Raw ARM PMU event configs can also be requested as hexadecimal values such as
\fB0x11\fR.

Short reads, read errors, counter resets, and intervals with no PMU running
time make that CPU unavailable for the interval and establish a new baseline.
Summary PMU data is available only when every tracked CPU has a valid interval.
IPC is available only when the active list contains both named events
\fBcycles\fR and \fBinstructions\fR and the interval cycle count is nonzero.

Busy/Idle percentages are not produced by a hidden backend object. Raw
authoritative counters are captured once per interval, then converted into
interval deltas in the aggregation layer, using the selected busy-source policy
to choose between \fB/proc/stat\fR and \fB/proc/schedstat\fR per CPU.

The schedstat reader accepts known versions 10 through 17, requires all nine
CPU fields, and reads task runtime from field 7. Unknown versions or invalid
records fall back to procstat through the per-CPU busy-source policy.

.SH OUTPUT MODEL
Machine-readable output uses schema version 8. Unavailable numeric and string
data is rendered as \fB-\fR in text, \fBnull\fR in JSON, and an empty CSV cell.
A real zero remains numeric zero. \fBBoost\fR is a JSON boolean when available
and uses 1 or 0 in text/CSV. CSV can represent summary, package, and CPU data
in one stream. Summary-only CSV uses a \fBScope\fR column containing \fBSUM\fR.
Mixed output uses the identity columns
\fBScope,CPU,Package\fR and \fBSUM\fR, \fBPKG\fR, or \fBCPU\fR rows; fields are
prefixed with \fBsummary.\fR, \fBpackage.\fR, or \fBcpu.\fR. A package-only
selection such as \fB-s pkg_freq_mhz\fR uses a compact \fBPackage\fR layout;
the physical package identity is serialized once. Compact CSV data columns use
the canonical JSON field keys, and compact PMU columns use \fBpmu.<event>\fR.
Schema 8 JSON and CSV also
include the measured sampling window in \fBduration_us\fR and nanosecond Unix
time in \fBtimestamp_ns\fR. The existing \fBtimestamp\fR remains whole seconds,
and \fBtimestamp_iso\fR is RFC 3339 with nine fractional digits.

.SS Summary-scope fields
Typical summary fields include:

.IP \(bu 2
\fBFreq\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUncoreFreq\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIdle%\fR and \fBBusy%\fR derived from the selected busy-source policy
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIOWait%\fR derived from \fB/proc/stat\fR iowait accounting
.IP \(bu 2
idle-state residency columns labelled from cpuidle \fBstateN/name\fR (for
example \fBLPI-0\fR, \fBLPI-1\fR) when cpuidle data is available
.IP \(bu 2
up to eight \fBLPI-*\fR columns are visible; deeper or unavailable states are
folded into the deepest visible usable residual bucket
.IP \(bu 2
visible \fBLPI-*\fR columns are display-oriented: shallower usable states keep
their raw cpuidle residency, while the deepest visible usable state becomes a
residual bucket so that visible \fBLPI-*\fR columns sum to \fBIdle%\fR
.IP \(bu 2
if a deeper state is disabled, unavailable, or beyond the visible column limit,
the residual is assigned to the deepest remaining visible usable state
.IP \(bu 2
if a visible usable cpuidle state lacks a current value or previous baseline,
all visible split-state values for that CPU are unavailable for the interval;
the recovered sample establishes a new baseline
.IP \(bu 2
usage rates for missing, disabled, or otherwise unusable cpuidle states are
unavailable rather than zero
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPower\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTemp0\fR ... \fBTemp3\fR when available
.IP \(bu 2
\fBEnergy\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMemBW\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCtxSw\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIRQs\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSoftIRQs\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIPC\fR
.IP \(bu 2
aggregated PMU counters
.IP \(bu 2
summary \fBLPI-*\fR values are averages of the displayed per-CPU idle-state
columns, not a separate whole-machine residual calculation

.SS CPU-scope fields
Typical CPU fields include:

.IP \(bu 2
\fBCPU\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPkg\fR, \fBCore\fR, \fBNode\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFreq\fR, \fBMin\fR, \fBMax\fR, \fBGovernor\fR, \fBBoost\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIdle%\fR and \fBBusy%\fR derived from the selected busy-source policy
.IP \(bu 2
idle-state residency columns labelled from cpuidle \fBstateN/name\fR when
cpuidle data is available
.IP \(bu 2
the deepest visible \fBLPI-*\fR column may therefore be residual-adjusted
rather than a pure raw cpuidle percentage
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIPC\fR when requested
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTemp\fR
.IP \(bu 2
per-CPU PMU counters

.SS Package-scope fields
Package rows include the package ID, tracked CPU count, current sampled
frequency averaged across valid tracked CPUs,
Busy/Idle, and I/O-wait fields selected for that package. They are enabled by
\fB-s pkg\fR, an exact package field, or \fB-a\fR. With \fB--cpu\fR, aggregate
sections are suppressed only when they would be implicitly mixed with CPU
rows; an explicit package-only selection uses the filtered tracked CPU set.

.SH DATA SOURCES AND FORMULAS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFreq\fR: \fBcpuinfo_cur_freq / 1000\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMin\fR / \fBMax\fR: \fBscaling_min_freq\fR and \fBscaling_max_freq\fR,
converted from kHz to MHz
.IP \(bu 2
Summary/package \fBFreq\fR: arithmetic mean of the current readable per-CPU
\fBcpuinfo_cur_freq\fR samples in that scope. This is a cpufreq sample, not a
time average, hardware-counter-derived effective frequency, or busy frequency
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUncoreFreq\fR: a unique readable
\fB/sys/class/devfreq/<device>/cur_freq\fR whose device name looks like an
uncore/interconnect/fabric clock source, converted from Hz to MHz and exported
as \fBuncore_freq\fR. If the devfreq layout is ambiguous, armstat hides
\fBuncore_freq\fR instead of guessing
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIdle%\fR: from the selected busy-source policy; with \fBprocstat\fR it is
\fB/proc/stat idle delta / interval delta * 100\fR, while with
\fBschedstat\fR it is \fB100 - Busy%\fR from per-CPU runtime accounting, and
\fBtask-clock\fR is a compatibility alias for the same formula. \fBIdle%\fR is
the authoritative total idle budget for the interval. Linux's iowait counter
is included in the procstat idle value
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIOWait%\fR: \fB/proc/stat iowait delta / interval delta * 100\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBusy%\fR: with \fBprocstat\fR it is \fB100 - Idle%\fR; with
\fBschedstat\fR it is \fBsched_runtime_delta_ns / wall_clock_delta_ns * 100\fR;
\fBtask-clock\fR is a compatibility alias for the same formula
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIOWait%\fR is reported independently as an explanatory subset of
\fBIdle%\fR and is not subtracted from \fBBusy%\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLPI-*\fR: cpuidle \fBstateN/time\fR delta divided by interval time; the
deepest visible usable state may be residual-adjusted so visible
\fBLPI-*\fR columns sum to \fBIdle%\fR. Raw cpuidle residency is used as the
idle-state breakdown input, while \fBIdle%\fR remains the authoritative total
idle budget. At most eight LPI columns are printed.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLPI-*_usage\fR: per-CPU cpuidle \fBstateN/usage\fR delta divided by the
measured interval in seconds. Counter failures and resets are unavailable;
recovery establishes a new baseline. cpuidle reads are selected independently
by visible state and counter family, so usage-only output does not read
\fBstateN/time\fR or require \fB/proc/stat\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPower\fR: package power from exactly one discovered
\fBpower_meter/power1_average\fR source
.IP \(bu 2
\fBEnergy\fR: interval average power multiplied by interval duration
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMemBW\fR: delta of exactly one discovered platform-specific
\fBmem_bytes_read\fR counter divided by interval time, reported as MiB/s
(bytes per second divided by 1024 squared)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCtxSw\fR: \fB/proc/stat ctxt\fR delta
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIRQs\fR: \fB/proc/stat intr\fR delta
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSoftIRQs\fR: \fB/proc/stat softirq\fR delta
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIPC\fR: \fBinstructions / cycles\fR

.SH PLATFORM NOTES
The current implementation assumes a platform model where:

.IP \(bu 2
package power requires exactly one \fBpower_meter/power1_average\fR candidate
.IP \(bu 2
memory bandwidth requires exactly one \fBmem_bytes_read\fR candidate
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthermal_zoneN/temp\fR maps directly to \fBTempN\fR and NUMA/Vdie node
\fBN\fR under the current \fBthermal-zone-index\fR summary-temperature policy

As a result:

.IP \(bu 2
\fBPower\fR is a \fBSUM\fR-scope field backed by a package-level source; it is
not emitted on \fBPKG\fR or CPU rows
.IP \(bu 2
CPU-row \fBTemp\fR is derived from the CPU's NUMA node temperature
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTemp0\fR through \fBTemp3\fR are summary fields shown for discovered
NUMA/Vdie zones. Zone numbering may be sparse, and signed temperature values
are preserved
.IP \(bu 2
\fB--probe\fR reports the effective \fBsummary_temp_policy\fR and package-power
and memory-bandwidth candidate counts. Multiple candidates are treated as
ambiguous and disabled instead of selecting the first directory entry
.IP \(bu 2
\fBARMSTAT_TEMP_POLICY=none\fR disables summary \fBTempN\fR discovery when the
platform does not follow the direct \fBthermal_zoneN -> TempN -> NUMA/Vdie N\fR
mapping. Accepted values are \fBthermal-zone-index\fR, \fBnone\fR, and
\fBdisabled\fR; any other value is a startup error
.IP \(bu 2
per-core power is not currently exposed

.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
There are no dedicated core aggregate rows yet; summary, per-package, and
per-CPU rows are implemented
.IP \(bu 2
Per-state idle columns require cpuidle support; when cpuidle is unavailable,
\fBIdle%\fR / \fBBusy%\fR continue through the selected procstat/schedstat
policy
.IP \(bu 2
The deepest visible idle-state column is a display-adjusted residual bucket
when needed, so it should not always be interpreted as a raw \fBstateN/time\fR
percentage
.IP \(bu 2
Requested PMU / IPC fields may render as unavailable when perf access is not
possible; this is intentional and preferable to silently reporting zero
.IP \(bu 2
Transient frequency, cpuidle, power, temperature, memory-bandwidth, procstat,
and PMU read failures invalidate only dependent fields; recovered cumulative
sources are re-baselined before interval deltas resume
.IP \(bu 2
A procstat jiffy delta that cannot be represented safely in microseconds is
unavailable rather than wrapping into a plausible Busy/Idle percentage
.IP \(bu 2
Per-core power is not implemented
.IP \(bu 2
Linux CPU IDs above 1023 cannot be represented by the current fixed-size
sampling arrays. armstat preserves the actual online count, prints a truncation
warning, and continues with representable online IDs
.IP \(bu 2
PMU monitoring usually requires root privileges or permissive perf settings

.SH EXIT STATUS
armstat returns 0 only after the requested operation and its stdout flush have
succeeded. It returns 1 for invalid arguments, initialization or collection
failure, an unusable effective field selection, and detected output write or
finalization failure. A closed downstream pipe is handled as \fBEPIPE\fR
rather than terminating through \fBSIGPIPE\fR, so resources are cleaned up
before armstat returns 1.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Default aggregate output:
.B armstat
.TP
Expand with per-CPU rows:
.B armstat -a
.TP
5-second interval:
.B armstat -i 5
.TP
10 iterations:
.B armstat -n 10
.TP
Summary-only:
.B armstat -S
.TP
Show package/core/node/frequency/idle:
.B armstat -s pkg,core,node,freq,idle
.TP
Enable PMU counters:
.B armstat -p cycles,instructions
.TP
Enable IPC:
.B armstat -I
.TP
JSON output:
.B armstat -f json
.TP
Export JSON to file:
.B armstat -f json -O armstat.json
.TP
Export CSV to file:
.B armstat -f csv -O armstat.csv
.TP
Plot an installed summary export:
.B armstat-plot-summary summary.json --preset power-temp
.TP
Plot selected CPUs from an installed export:
.B armstat-plot-cpu cpus.csv --cpu-filter 0-7 --preset busy
.TP
List built-in column groups and PMU event names:
.B armstat -l
.TP
Probe the current platform:
.B armstat --probe

.PP
Architecture, machine-readable exports, plotting, and release validation are
documented together in \fBREFERENCE.md\fR and \fBREFERENCE.zh-CN.md\fR.
When installed with \fBmake install\fR, plotting is exposed through
\fBarmstat-plot-summary\fR and \fBarmstat-plot-cpu\fR; their shared modules
are installed under \fBshare/armstat/\fR and reference documentation under
\fBshare/doc/armstat/\fR. Known field units are shown in field listings and
axis labels. Sample-based smoothing preserves unavailable samples as gaps;
CPUs or groups with no primary-field data in the selected window are reported
and skipped rather than producing empty legend entries. In a two-axis CPU
plot, an entity with no secondary-field data keeps its primary line while its
empty secondary line is reported and skipped.

